# PERIODIZATION ENGINE

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

The Periodization Engine organizes training over time.

It transforms isolated sessions into a coherent long-term development plan.

Performance is not built in one workout.

It is built through the intelligent sequencing of many workouts.

---

# Mission

The Periodization Engine answers one question.

What should the athlete train now,

to maximize future performance?

---

# Core Principle

Every session belongs to a larger plan.

No workout exists in isolation.

Every decision must support the current phase while preparing the next one.

---

# Philosophy

Adaptation requires variation.

Variation requires planning.

Planning requires objectives.

The Periodization Engine continuously balances:

Stress

↓

Recovery

↓

Adaptation

↓

Performance

---

# Time Horizons

The engine reasons simultaneously across multiple horizons.

Today's session

↓

Current week

↓

Training block

↓

Macrocycle

↓

Long-term athlete development

No decision should optimize one horizon while damaging another.

---

# Planning Levels

CAS recognizes four planning levels.

Session

↓

Microcycle

↓

Mesocycle

↓

Macrocycle

Each level has its own objectives.

---

# Session

The smallest planning unit.

Purpose:

Deliver one specific training stimulus.

---

# Microcycle

Usually one training week.

Purpose:

Organize stimulus distribution.

Balance recovery.

Coordinate physical qualities.

---

# Mesocycle

Typically four to eight weeks.

Purpose:

Develop one primary adaptation.

Examples:

Maximum Strength

Power

Hypertrophy

Aerobic Capacity

Competition Preparation

---

# Macrocycle

Several months.

Purpose:

Prepare the athlete for long-term objectives.

Competition.

Season.

Skill development.

Career progression.

---

# Planning Objectives

Every planning decision should answer:

Why this quality?

Why now?

Why before this?

Why before the next phase?

---

# Training Qualities

The engine organizes development of:

Maximum Strength

Explosive Strength

Power

Speed

Acceleration

Aerobic Capacity

Anaerobic Capacity

Muscular Endurance

Mobility

