---

# Priority System

Each training block defines:

Primary Quality

Secondary Quality

Maintenance Qualities

Only one primary adaptation should dominate.

---

# Adaptation Windows

Different adaptations require different durations.

Examples:

Neural adaptations

↓

Fast

Structural adaptations

↓

Moderate

Aerobic adaptations

↓

Long-term

Technical mastery

↓

Continuous

Planning respects these timelines.

---

# Load Distribution

Stress should fluctuate.

Avoid continuous progression.

Preferred model:

Build

↓

Build

↓

Build

↓

Deload

The exact pattern depends on the athlete.

---

# Deload

Deload is planned.

Not improvised.

Objectives:

Reduce accumulated fatigue.

Maintain movement quality.

Prepare future progression.

---

# Competition Planning

Competition changes priorities.

The engine progressively shifts toward:

Specificity

Freshness

Explosiveness

Technical sharpness

Recovery

Training volume usually decreases.

Training quality increases.

---

# Transition Phase

After major competition:

Recovery.

Physical regeneration.

Psychological recovery.

General movement.

Preparation for the next cycle.

---

# Individualization

Periodization adapts to:

Training age.

Competition frequency.

Recovery capacity.

Available time.

Equipment.

Life constraints.

Two athletes rarely share identical planning.

---

